# Initial Setup's Serial Ports page, ported from GCSViews/ConfigurationView/ConfigSerial.cs.
#
# The claim: Activate builds a row per port up to the highest SERIALn_BAUD - SERIAL1 to SERIAL7 on
# SITL - with the speed and protocol from the vehicle in combo boxes of the documented values, the
# set options bits' names and a Set Bitmask button, and the reboot note under them
# (ConfigSerial.cs:37-380). A change is written at once (:254-296, 467-491); a protocol change then
# applies SerialOptionRules.json, whose MAVLink2 rule sets the options to 0 and puts its comment
# in the note (:384-430); a bit ticked in the bitmask window is written and named in the options
# label (:327-353). Each change is proved by the vehicle's echo, and put back.
#
# The ports' names: the page object's first Activate reads @SYS/uarts.txt over MAVFTP - a plain
# read, behind a modal progress window - and builds the table when it has ended; each row is
# "SERIAL PORT n" with, under it, the second word of the file's SERIALn line (:77-161, 184-212).
# SITL's UARTs have no names, so every SITL line's second word is the first counter's, "TX=":
#
#   SERIAL1 TX=       0 RX=       0 TXBD=     0 RXBD=     0 not connected (tcp:2)
#
# and SERIAL1 to SERIAL8 are named "TX=", as Mission Planner names them on SITL. Shown again on
# the same screen, the page does not read the file again (_gotUARTNames).
#
# SERIAL5 is used because SITL leaves it without a protocol; a serial change takes effect only
# when the board reboots, and nothing here reboots it.
#
# The speeds and protocols come from the documentation fetched for the firmware flying: the
# bundled table lists them for SERIAL1 only, and the C# offers no box for a port whose
# documentation lists none.
#
# Needs SITL copter on tcp:127.0.0.1:5760 started from tools/sitl/params/copter.parm, whose serial
# ports are the firmware's defaults: SERIAL1 and 2 MAVLink2 at 57600, 3 and 4 GPS at 230400, 5 to 7
# none at 57600, every SERIALn_OPTIONS 0. Run as
#
#   tools/gui-test.sh tests/gui/config-serial.gui -- tcp:127.0.0.1:5760
#
# A run that stops half way can leave SERIAL5_PROTOCOL at 2, SERIAL5_BAUD at 115 or
# SERIAL5_OPTIONS at 1; the first expectations below then fail until a fresh SITL.

screen params
window 1600x1200
# A config.xml of its own, so the "Show me again?" boxes this script clicks are not already
# suppressed by the settings of the machine running it.
env MP_CONFIG_XML $WORK/config.xml
settle 6
# RawParamWarning, on the Full Parameter List's Activate (ConfigRawParams.cs:92): "Show me
# again?" unticked, so no later visit asks, and OK.
click raw-param-warning-again
click raw-param-warning-ok

click param-download
settle 15
expect params.held > 1000
expect params.value.SERIAL5_PROTOCOL -1
expect params.value.SERIAL5_BAUD 57
expect params.value.SERIAL5_OPTIONS 0
# The fetched documentation is read once the banner has named the firmware.
expect params.metadata.source ~ Copter

click tab-setup
expect config.serial.active false
click setup-page-ConfigMandatory
click setup-page-ConfigSerial
# Time for the read: a reset, an open and nine 80-byte reads of SITL's 712 bytes.
settle 3
expect setup.page ConfigSerial
expect config.serial.active true

# The names, read and the window closed.
expect config.serial.uarts loaded
expect config.serial.uarts.names 8
expect config.serial.progress none
expect config.serial.1.name TX=
expect config.serial.7.name TX=
expect config.serial.7.label SERIAL PORT 7 TX=

# What Activate built.
expect config.serial.ports 7
expect config.serial.rows 7
expect config.serial.note Note: Changes to the serial port settings will not take effect until the board is rebooted.
expect config.serial.1.label SERIAL PORT 1 TX=
expect config.serial.1.baud 57
expect config.serial.1.baud.text 57600
expect config.serial.1.protocol 2
expect config.serial.1.protocol.text MAVLink2
expect config.serial.3.protocol.text GPS
expect config.serial.3.baud.text 230400
expect config.serial.5.protocol -1
expect config.serial.5.protocol.text None
expect config.serial.5.baud.text 57600
expect config.serial.5.bitmask true
expect config.serial.write none

# The speed: 115200, then 57600 again.
click serial-SERIAL5_BAUD
click serial-SERIAL5_BAUD-115
settle 3
expect config.serial.5.baud 115
expect config.serial.5.baud.text 115200
expect config.serial.write SERIAL5_BAUD 115 accepted
expect params.value.SERIAL5_BAUD 115
click serial-SERIAL5_BAUD
click serial-SERIAL5_BAUD-57
settle 3
expect config.serial.write SERIAL5_BAUD 57 accepted
expect params.value.SERIAL5_BAUD 57

# The protocol: MAVLink2, whose rule writes the options 0 - which they already are, so the set is
# not sent - and puts its comment in the note.
# MAVLink2 is row 30 of the fetched documentation's list (alphabetical after None), one past the
# thirty the list shows: a notch of the wheel brings it into view.
click serial-SERIAL5_PROTOCOL
expect config.serial.list.top 0
scroll serial-SERIAL5_PROTOCOL-list down 1
expect config.serial.list.top 3
click serial-SERIAL5_PROTOCOL-2
settle 3
expect config.serial.5.protocol 2
expect config.serial.5.protocol.text MAVLink2
expect params.value.SERIAL5_PROTOCOL 2
expect config.serial.write SERIAL5_OPTIONS 0 unchanged
expect config.serial.note SERIAL5 : If connecting a Mavlink sensor, consider setting 'Do not forward Mavlink to/from'
# And back to none, which has no rule.
# Opened on MAVLink2, the top row is 1 and None, row 0, is just out of view.
click serial-SERIAL5_PROTOCOL
expect config.serial.list.top 1
scroll serial-SERIAL5_PROTOCOL-list up 1
expect config.serial.list.top 0
click serial-SERIAL5_PROTOCOL--1
settle 3
expect config.serial.5.protocol -1
expect config.serial.write SERIAL5_PROTOCOL -1 accepted
expect params.value.SERIAL5_PROTOCOL -1

# The bitmask window: InvertRX ticked writes 1 and names it; unticked writes 0.
click serial-SERIAL5_OPTIONS-bitmask
expect config.serial.dialog SERIAL5_OPTIONS
expect config.serial.dialog.checked none
click serial-bitmask-SERIAL5_OPTIONS-0
settle 3
expect config.serial.dialog.checked 0
expect config.serial.write SERIAL5_OPTIONS 1 accepted
expect params.value.SERIAL5_OPTIONS 1
expect config.serial.5.options InvertRX
click serial-bitmask-SERIAL5_OPTIONS-0
settle 3
expect config.serial.write SERIAL5_OPTIONS 0 accepted
expect params.value.SERIAL5_OPTIONS 0
click serial-bitmask-SERIAL5_OPTIONS-close
expect config.serial.dialog none
expect config.serial.message none
expect config.serial.writes.pending 0

# Choosing another page hides this one.
click setup-page-ConfigFlightModes
expect setup.page ConfigFlightModes
expect config.serial.active false

# And shown again: the same page object, which has its names already - the table is built at
# once, with no read and no window.
click setup-page-ConfigSerial
expect setup.page ConfigSerial
expect config.serial.active true
expect config.serial.progress none
expect config.serial.uarts loaded
expect config.serial.1.label SERIAL PORT 1 TX=
